nks/0xv9uz7g8af6d.js\"],\"LessonClient\"]\n11:T871,# Variables and Data Types\n\nIn PowerShell, variables are storage containers for data. Every variable name starts with a `$` sign, and you create one simply by assigning a value to it.\n\n## Creating and Assigning Variables\n\n```powershell\n$name = \"Alice\"\n$age = 30\n$isAdmin = $true\n```\n\nNo declaration keyword is needed — variables spring into existence on first assignment. Variable names are **case-insensitive**, so `$Name` and `$name` refer to the same variable.\n\n> **Key Concept:** PowerShell uses **automatic type detection**. When you assign `42`, the variable becomes an `[int]`. Assign `\"hello\"`, and it becomes a `[string]`. You can check a variable's type with `.GetType()`.\n\n```powershell\n$count = 42\n$count.GetType().Name   # Int32\n\n$count = \"hello\"\n$count.GetType().Name   # String\n```\n\n## Common Data Types\n\n| Type         | Example                          |\n|--------------|----------------------------------|\n| `[string]`   | `\"Hello World\"`                  |\n| `[int]`      | `42`                             |\n| `[bool]`     | `$true`, `$false`                |\n| `[array]`    | `@(1, 2, 3)`                    |\n| `[datetime]` | `Get-Date`                       |\n\n## Type Casting\n\nYou can force a variable to a specific type by placing the type accelerator before the variable name or the value:\n\n```powershell\n[int]$port = \"8080\"          # converts string \"8080\" to integer 8080\n[string]$code = 404          # converts integer 404 to string \"404\"\n[datetime]$date = \"2025-01-15\"  # converts string to DateTime object\n```\n\nOnce a variable is constrained with a type, it rejects incompatible values:\n\n```powershell\n[int]$number = 10\n$number = \"hello\"   # Error — \"hello\" cannot convert to int\n```\n\n## The $null Value\n\n`$null` represents the absence of a value. Any variable that has never been assigned is `$null`:\n\n```powershell\n$undefined -eq $null   # True\n```\n\n> **Key Concept:** Always place `$null` on the left side of comparisons (`$null -eq $var`) to avoid unexpected behavior when `$var` is an array.\n\nUse `$null` to explicitly clear a variable:\n\n```powershell\n$data = \"something\"\n$data = $null   # variable now has no value\n```\n4:[\"$\",\"div\",null,{\"className\":\"min-h-screen bg-zinc-950 text-zinc-100\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"header\",null,{\"className\":\"sticky top-0 z-10 border-b border-zinc-800/60 bg-zinc-950/80 backdrop-blur-xl\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"div\",null,{\"className\":\"mx-auto flex max-w-4xl items-center justify-between px-6 py-3.5\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"$Lf\",null,{\"href\":\"/module/fundamentals\",\"className\":\"inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 text-sm text-zinc-500 transition-colors hover:text-zinc-300\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"svg\",null,{\"className\":\"h-4 w-4\",\"fill\":\"none\",\"viewBox\":\"0 0 24 24\",\"strokeWidth\":2,\"stroke\":\"currentColor\",\"children\":[\"$\",\"path\",null,{\"strokeLinecap\":\"round\",\"strokeLinejoin\":\"round\",\"d\":\"M10.5 19.5L3 12m0 0l7.5-7.5M3 12h18\"}]}],\"PowerShell Fundamentals\"]}],[\"$\",\"div\",null,{\"className\":\"flex items-center gap-2\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"$Lf\",null,{\"href\":\"/module/fundamentals/what-is-powershell\",\"className\":\"flex h-8 w-8 items-center justify-center rounded-lg border border-zinc-800 text-zinc-400 transition-colors hover:border-zinc-700 hover:text-zinc-200\",\"title\":\"What is PowerShell?\",\"children\":[\"$\",\"svg\",null,{\"className\":\"h-4 w-4\",\"fill\":\"none\",\"viewBox\":\"0 0 24 24\",\"strokeWidth\":2,\"stroke\":\"currentColor\",\"children\":[\"$\",\"path\",null,{\"strokeLinecap\":\"round\",\"strokeLinejoin\":\"round\",\"d\":\"M15.75 19.5L8.25 12l7.5-7.5\"}]}]}],[\"$\",\"span\",null,{\"className\":\"text-xs tabular-nums text-zinc-500\",\"children\":[2,\" / \",9]}],[\"$\",\"$Lf\",null,{\"href\":\"/module/fundamentals/strings-arrays-hashtables\",\"className\":\"flex h-8 w-8 items-center justify-center rounded-lg border border-zinc-800 text-zinc-400 transition-colors hover:border-zinc-700 hover:text-zinc-200\",\"title\":\"Strings, Arrays, and Hashtables\",\"children\":[\"$\",\"svg\",null,{\"className\":\"h-4 w-4\",\"fill\":\"none\",\"viewBox\":\"0 0 24 24\",\"strokeWidth\":2,\"stroke\":\"currentColor\",\"children\":[\"$\",\"path\",null,{\"strokeLinecap\":\"round\",\"strokeLinejoin\":\"round\",\"d\":\"M8.25 4.5l7.5 7.5-7.5 7.5\"}]}]}]]}]]}],[\"$\",\"div\",null,{\"className\":\"mx-auto max-w-4xl px-6 pb-4\",\"children\":[\"$\",\"h1\",null,{\"className\":\"text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight\",\"children\":\"Variables and Data Types\"}]}]]}],[\"$\",\"$L10\",null,{\"lesson\":{\"meta\":{\"title\":\"Variables and Data Types\",\"description\":\"Introduction to PowerShell variables, types, and type casting.\",\"slug\":\"variables-and-types\",\"order\":2,\"prerequisites\":[\"m1-l1\"]},\"content\":\"$11\",\"exercises\":[{\"id\":\"m1-l2-ex1\",\"type\":\"syntax-check\",\"prompt\":\"Create a variable called $greeting and assign it the value \\\"Hello, World!\\\".\",\"hints\":[\"Variable names in PowerShell start with $.\",\"Use the = operator to assign a value.\",\"Strings are wrapped in quotes.\"],\"validation\":[{\"rule\":\"regex\",\"pattern\":\"\\\\$greeting\\\\s*=\",\"hintOnFail\":\"Name the variable $greeting and use = to assign a value.\"},{\"rule\":\"regex\",\"pattern\":\"[\\\"']Hello,\\\\s*World![\\\"']\",\"hintOnFail\":\"Assign the string \\\"Hello, World!\\\" as the value.\"}],\"solution\":\"$$greeting = \\\"Hello, World!\\\"\",\"explanation\":\"Variables are created by assigning a value with the $ prefix and = operator. No declaration keyword is needed.\"},{\"id\":\"m1-l2-ex2\",\"type\":\"syntax-check\",\"prompt\":\"Create a variable $port constrained to the [int] type and assign it the value 443.\",\"hints\":[\"Place the type accelerator before the variable name.\",\"Type accelerators use square brackets, like [int].\",\"Try: [int]$port = 443\"],\"validation\":[{\"rule\":\"regex\",\"pattern\":\"\\\\[int\\\\]\\\\s*\\\\$port\",\"hintOnFail\":\"Use [int] before $port to constrain its type.\"},{\"rule\":\"contains\",\"value\":\"443\",\"hintOnFail\":\"Assign the value 443.\"}],\"solution\":\"[int]$port = 443\",\"explanation\":\"Placing [int] before the variable name constrains it to only accept integer values. Assigning a non-convertible value later will cause an error.\"},{\"id\":\"m1-l2-ex3\",\"type\":\"syntax-check\",\"prompt\":\"Cast the string \\\"2025-06-15\\\" to a [datetime] and store it in a variable called $releaseDate.\",\"hints\":[\"Use [datetime] as the type accelerator.\",\"Place it before the variable name or before the value.\",\"Try: [datetime]$releaseDate = \\\"2025-06-15\\\"\"],\"validation\":[{\"rule\":\"regex\",\"pattern\":\"\\\\[datetime\\\\]\",\"hintOnFail\":\"Use [datetime] to cast the string to a DateTime object.\"},{\"rule\":\"regex\",\"pattern\":\"\\\\$releaseDate\\\\s*=\",\"hintOnFail\":\"Store the result in a variable named $releaseDate.\"},{\"rule\":\"contains\",\"value\":\"2025-06-15\",\"hintOnFail\":\"Use the date string \\\"2025-06-15\\\".\"}],\"solution\":\"[datetime]$releaseDate = \\\"2025-06-15\\\"\",\"explanation\":\"PowerShell can parse common date string formats and convert them to [datetime] objects automatically when you specify the type.\"},{\"id\":\"m1-l2-ex4\",\"type\":\"syntax-check\",\"prompt\":\"Write an expression that checks whether the variable $result is $null. Place $null on the left side of the comparison, as recommended.\",\"hints\":[\"Use the -eq comparison operator.\",\"Best practice is to put $null on the left: $null -eq $variable.\",\"This avoids unexpected behavior when the variable is an array.\"],\"validation\":[{\"rule\":\"regex\",\"pattern\":\"\\\\$null\\\\s+-eq\\\\s+\\\\$result\",\"hintOnFail\":\"Place $null on the left side: $null -eq $result.\"},{\"rule\":\"forbidden\",\"value\":\"$$result -eq\",\"hintOnFail\":\"Put $null on the left side of the comparison, not $result.\"}],\"solution\":\"$$null -eq $result\",\"explanation\":\"Placing $null on the left side prevents unexpected behavior when $result is an array, because -eq against an array filters elements rather than performing a simple equality check.\"}],\"moduleSlug\":\"fundamentals\"}}],\"$L12\"]}]\n12:[\"$\",\"footer\",null,{\"className\":\"border-t border-zinc-800/60\",\"children\":[\"$\",\"div\",null,{\"className\":\"mx-auto flex max-w-4xl items-center justify-between px-6 py-6\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"$Lf\",null,{\"href\":\"/module/fundamentals/what-is-powershell\",\"className\":\"group flex items-center gap-2 text-sm text-zinc-400 transition-colors hover:text-zinc-200\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"svg\",null,{\"className\":\"h-4 w-4 transition-transform group-hover:-translate-x-0.5\",\"fill\":\"none\",\"viewBox\":\"0 0 24 24\",\"strokeWidth\":2,\"stroke\":\"currentColor\",\"children\":[\"$\",\"path\",null,{\"strokeLinecap\":\"round\",\"strokeLinejoin\":\"round\",\"d\":\"M10.5 19.5L3 12m0 0l7.5-7.5M3 12h18\"}]}],\"What is PowerShell?\"]}],[\"$\",\"$Lf\",null,{\"href\":\"/module/fundamentals/strings-arrays-hashtables\",\"className\":\"group flex items-center gap-2 text-sm text-zinc-400 transition-colors hover:text-zinc-200\",\"children\":[\"Strings, Arrays, and Hashtables\",[\"$\",\"svg\",null,{\"className\":\"h-4 w-4 transition-transform group-hover:translate-x-0.5\",\"fill\":\"none\",\"viewBox\":\"0 0 24 24\",\"strokeWidth\":2,\"stroke\":\"currentColor\",\"children\":[\"$\",\"path\",null,{\"strokeLinecap\":\"round\",\"strokeLinejoin\":\"round\",\"d\":\"M13.5 4.5L21 12m0 0l-7.5 7.5M21 12H3\"}]}]]}]]}]}]\na:[[\"$\",\"meta\",\"0\",{\"charSet\":\"utf-8\"}],[\"$\",\"meta\",\"1\",{\"name\":\"viewport\",\"content\":\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1\"}]]\n13:I[27201,[\"/_next/static/chunks/01xlw8hd842-c.js\",\"/_next/static/chunks/0d3shmwh5_nmn.js\"],\"IconMark\"]\n7:null\nc:[[\"$\",\"title\",\"0\",{\"children\":\"PowerShell Training\"}],[\"$\",\"meta\",\"1\",{\"nam